<section class="reading wrap"><span class="eyebrow">THE WEB WE WANT TO SPEND TIME ON</span><h1 class="page-title">A small web.<br><em>A wide-open door.</em></h1><p class="lead">Superapp is a collection of useful little tools for people who would rather make a corner of the internet than rent a place in a feed.</p><div class="manifesto"><article><b>01</b><div><h2>Canadian roots. Everyone welcome.</h2><p>Support Canadian makers, independent businesses, and community networks. No nationality requirement, purity tests, or automatic “made in Canada” labels.</p></div></article><article><b>02</b><div><h2>Your words belong to you.</h2><p>Public pages use open web formats. Export your data. Read feeds in your own reader. Run the GPL-licensed code on your own Linux server.</p></div></article><article><b>03</b><div><h2>Contribute what you can.</h2><p>No subscription tiers or paywalls. Voluntary contributions never buy more reach or a better place in the directory.</p></div></article><article><b>04</b><div><h2>Useful beats addictive.</h2><p>Chronological updates, no like counts, no follower race, no advertising or analytics scripts. A good tool helps you finish and get on with your day.</p></div></article><article><b>05</b><div><h2>Old internet. New care.</h2><p>Guestbooks and /now pages. Runbooks and RSS. Thoughtful moderation and privacy defaults. A place for Linux tinkerers, archivists, wanderers, and the just-curious.</p></div></article></div><div class="note"><strong>About the inspiration</strong><p>The small-service bundle was inspired by <a href="https://omg.lol">omg.lol</a>. Superapp is an independent project with original code and design, not affiliated with omg.lol. Its focus is Canadian discovery, self-hosting, field notes, portability, and voluntary support.</p></div><h2>Where this instance lives</h2><p><?=esc(cfg('hosting_note'))?></p><p>Canadian hosting is a deployment choice, not something a .ca domain guarantees. Operators should state where the application, database, backups and payment provider process data.</p></section>
<?php elseif($page==='privacy'): ?>
<section class="reading wrap"><span class="eyebrow">PLAIN WORDS, OPEN DOORS</span><h1>Privacy & community notes.</h1><h2>What the app stores</h2><p>Your username, private email address, password hash, public profile, entries, moderation records, guestbook messages, and hashed API tokens. A necessary session cookie keeps you logged in. Rate limits keep temporary hashes derived from the connecting IP address; run the cleanup command daily. The web server may keep access logs, which the operator must manage separately.</p><h2>What is public</h2><p>Your profile, /now and /uses pages are public by their address. New entries are private by default. Public entries appear in feeds and community pages when discovery is enabled. Unlisted entries can be read by anyone with their URL; they are not a safe place for secrets. Approved guestbook messages and directory submissions are public.</p><h2>No advertising or tracking scripts</h2><p>The app loads its own assets only. It does not fetch your saved URLs or remotely embed images. Following external links or a contribution link takes you to a separate service with its own privacy practices.</p><h2>Leaving and backups</h2><p>You can export your data and delete your account in Settings. Deletion removes active database records, including your submissions and guestbook messages. Operator backups and server logs may retain older copies until their retention period expires. Ask the instance operator for that period.</p><h2>Be a good neighbour</h2><p>No harassment, hate, doxxing, impersonation, scams, spam, malware, or publishing other people’s private information. Share material you have the right to share. Credit sources. Respect physical access rules when sharing field notes. Directory claims need a relevant source, and approval is not certification.</p><h2>Reports and moderation</h2><p>Signed-in members can report individual entries. Profile owners review guestbook messages. Instance administrators can review directory submissions and reports, and suspend accounts. Contact the operator for other concerns or password recovery.</p><?php if(cfg('contact_url')):?><a class="button outline" href="<?=esc(web_url(cfg('contact_url')))?>">Contact this instance’s operator ↗</a><?php else:?><p class="note">The operator has not configured a contact link. This instance should remain a closed pilot until an appropriate contact and retention policy are published.</p><?php endif?><p class="help">These describe this software’s current behaviour. The operator is responsible for publishing service-specific terms and retention details before a public launch.</p></section>
